\section{Structural zeros}{

Let \code{group} be a discrete variable with two or more levels. A taxon has
a structural zero in a group if it is absent, or nearly absent, from that
group while being present in at least one other group. For instance, suppose
there are three groups: g1, g2, and g3. If the counts of taxon A are 0 in g1
and nonzero in g2 and g3, then taxon A has a structural zero in g1. Taxon A
is then declared to be differentially abundant between g1 and g2 and between
g1 and g3, and consequently it is declared to be differentially abundant with
respect to the group variable.

Structural zeros are detected when \code{struc_zero = TRUE}, using the
criteria stated in section 3.2 of
\href{https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2017.02114}{ANCOM-II}. Let
\eqn{\hat{p}_{jk}}{p_jk} denote the observed prevalence of taxon \eqn{j} in
group \eqn{k}, that is, the proportion of samples of group \eqn{k} in which
taxon \eqn{j} has a nonzero count, and let \eqn{n_k} denote the number of
samples of group \eqn{k}. Under \code{neg_lb = FALSE}, taxon \eqn{j} is
declared to have a structural zero in group \eqn{k} when
\deqn{\hat{p}_{jk} = 0.}{p_jk = 0.}
Under \code{neg_lb = TRUE}, taxon \eqn{j} is declared to have a structural
zero in group \eqn{k} when the above equation holds or when the asymptotic
lower bound of the prevalence is nonpositive, that is, when
\deqn{\hat{p}_{jk} - 1.96 \sqrt{\hat{p}_{jk} (1 - \hat{p}_{jk}) / n_k} \le 0.}{p_jk - 1.96 * sqrt(p_jk * (1 - p_jk) / n_k) <= 0.}
Setting \code{neg_lb = TRUE} is recommended when the number of samples per
group is relatively large, for example greater than 30.

The three differential abundance functions treat these taxa differently.
\code{\link{ancom}} removes the taxa with structural zeros in at least one
group from the log-ratio comparisons, and reports them in \code{res} with
\code{W} set to infinity and the detection indicators set to TRUE.
\code{\link{ancombc}} retains these taxa in the primary result, and sets
their standard errors, p-values, and adjusted p-values to 0 and
\code{diff_abn} to TRUE for each level of the \code{group} variable whose
structural zero status differs from that of the reference level.
\code{\link{ancombc2}} excludes these taxa from the primary result and
reports them in \code{zero_ind}.
}

\section{Mixed directional false discovery rate (mdFDR)}{

The pairwise directional test and the Dunnett's type of test compare the
levels of the \code{group} variable within each taxon and assign a direction
to each comparison that is declared significant. Let \eqn{S} denote the
number of comparisons declared significant over all taxa, and let \eqn{V}
denote the number of these comparisons that are either null or assigned the
wrong direction. The mixed directional false discovery rate is
\deqn{\mbox{mdFDR} = E \left\{ \frac{V}{\max(S, 1)} \right\},}{mdFDR = E[V / max(S, 1)],}
and it combines the errors due to multiple testing over taxa, multiple
pairwise comparisons within a taxon, and the direction assigned within a
comparison.

\code{\link{ancombc2}} controls the mdFDR at the level \eqn{\alpha} specified
by \code{alpha} through a two-stage procedure. In the first stage, the taxa
are screened by the global test of the \code{group} variable, whose p-values
are adjusted by the Benjamini-Hochberg procedure at level \eqn{\alpha}; let
\eqn{R} denote the number of taxa rejected and let \eqn{d} denote the number
of taxa tested. In the second stage, the p-values of the pairwise or
Dunnett's type of comparisons of each rejected taxon are adjusted by the
familywise error rate controlling procedure specified in
\code{fwer_ctrl_method}, applied within the taxon at level
\deqn{R \alpha / d.}{R * alpha / d.}
A taxon that is not rejected in the first stage has all of its comparisons
assigned a p-value of 1.
}

